            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Finds the largest value in a tensor - in the whole tensor, or in each row or each column, together with WHERE it was found.\n\nUse it for the peak of a signal, the loudest band of a spectrum, the fastest joint in a frame, the brightest pixel. With a dim it also says which row or column the peak was in, so \"how big\" and \"which one\" come from one node and cannot disagree.\n\nTHE dim ARGUMENT:\nWith no argument the WHOLE tensor is reduced to one value. A number typed after the name (t.max 0) chooses a dim to reduce along and adds a dim inlet and a second outlet for the indices to the node - it is the argument that creates them, so a node made without one cannot be given a dim later (retype it). For a table of rows and columns, dim 0 reduces DOWN each column (one answer per column) and dim 1 ALONG each row (one answer per row). The dim named is the one that disappears: a 6 by 8 tensor gives 8 answers on dim 0 and 6 on dim 1. Negative numbers count from the end: -1 is the last dim.\n\nNOT THE SAME AS t.maximum:\nt.max REDUCES - a tensor goes in and one value (or one per row or column) comes out. t.maximum compares two tensors element by element and keeps the larger at every position.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.max <dim: int>\nDefault: no dim - the whole tensor, and only one outlet.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.max 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor. Every one that arrives is searched and the answer sent - this is the only inlet that makes the node send. A list or a NumPy array is first turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu; a tensor is used as it is, on its own device, and the answer stays there. An empty tensor is ignored: nothing is sent.\nIf the tensor holds a NaN the answer is NaN (a NaN counts as largest).\n\ndim (inlet, only when an argument was given):\nA whole number. Changing it does NOT send by itself; the next tensor uses it. A dim the tensor does not have (2 or more for a table, or 1 for a 1-D tensor) is an error: the console prints \"Dimension out of range\" and nothing is sent.\nThere is no keep_dims option on this node.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmax tensor:\nWith no dim, one value (a 0-dimensional tensor). With a dim, a tensor of largest values with that dim removed. Same dtype as the input.\n\nmax indices (only with a dim):\nFor each answer, where along the dim it was found (int64): at dim 0 the row of each column's largest, at dim 1 the column. When several share the largest value the first is given. It is sent just before the values. For the position in the WHOLE tensor use t.argmax.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.rand makes 6 rows by 8 columns of values between 0 and 1, written in the cells; click its button for new ones. The plain t.max gives the largest of all 48. The slider sets the dim of the second node: it goes through 't bang int', so the number reaches the dim inlet first and the bang then makes the var re-send the held tensor - a dim change alone would not send. t.info shows the shape of the answer: [8] for dim 0, [6] for dim 1. At dim 0 the strip holds\neach column's largest value and the list the row it was found in;\nat dim 1, each row's and the column.\n\nRELATED:\nt.min finds the smallest. t.argmax gives only the position of the largest. t.median and t.mean summarise the middle. t.maximum is the element-wise version. np.amax is the NumPy reduction.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
